<p>Every Emacs user knows that pressing <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-p</code> in the minibuffer goes back through
the history of the things they’ve typed there before. That’s one of the first
things you learn, right after <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C-g</code>. And if you go back too far you can press
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> to go forward again. End of story, right?</p>

<p>Not quite! What happens if you press <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> right away, when there’s nothing to
go forward to? You might expect an error, but instead Emacs will often insert
something useful. That’s what the Emacs manual calls “future history” - a list
of things that the command guessed you might want to type next. <sup id="fnref:1" role="doc-noteref"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote" rel="footnote">1</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="a-few-examples">A Few Examples</h2>

<p>Here are a few built-in commands where <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> does something handy:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C-x C-f</code> (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">find-file</code>) - inserts the file name at point (if there’s one) and
after it the name of the file you’re visiting, which is great when you want
to open one of its siblings</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-%</code> (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">query-replace</code>) - inserts the symbol at point and after it the last
thing you searched for with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">isearch</code></li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-s o</code> (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">occur</code>) - the same suggestions, plus the active region</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-!</code> (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">shell-command</code>) - inserts the name of the file you’re visiting (or
the file at point in Dired), which is super handy for commands that operate
on the current file</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C-x b</code> (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">switch-to-buffer</code>) - inserts the default buffer and afterwards
keeps going through the rest of the buffers</li>
</ul>

<p>The last one shows something that applies to every command that uses
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">completing-read</code>. When you run out of default values, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> starts going through
the completion candidates themselves. So in a pinch <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> is a poor man’s
candidate cycling, even if you’re not using any fancy completion framework.</p>

<p><strong>Tip:</strong> A lot of prompts show their default value (e.g. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Describe function
(default car):</code>) and most people just press <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">RET</code> to accept it. If the default is
almost what you want, though, press <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> to insert it into the minibuffer and
edit it there.</p>

<h2 id="using-it-in-your-own-commands">Using it in Your Own Commands</h2>

<p>If you’re writing Emacs Lisp you get all of this pretty much for free. The
functions that read from the minibuffer (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">read-string</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">read-from-minibuffer</code>,
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">completing-read</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">read-shell-command</code> and friends) accept a default value,
and that default value can also be a list. The first element is the default and
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> will go over the rest of them in order:</p>

<div class="language-emacs-lisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">defun</span> <span class="nv">my-search</span> <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">term</span><span class="p">)</span>
  <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">interactive</span>
   <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">list</span> <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">read-string</span>
          <span class="s">"Search for: "</span> <span class="no">nil</span> <span class="no">nil</span>
          <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">delq</span> <span class="no">nil</span>
                <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">list</span> <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">thing-at-point</span> <span class="ss">'symbol</span> <span class="no">t</span><span class="p">)</span>
                      <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">add-log-current-defun</span><span class="p">)</span>
                      <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">and</span> <span class="nv">buffer-file-name</span>
                           <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">file-name-base</span> <span class="nv">buffer-file-name</span><span class="p">)))))))</span>
  <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">message</span> <span class="s">"Searching for %s"</span> <span class="nv">term</span><span class="p">))</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Here <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> will first offer the symbol at point, then the name of the current
function and finally the name of the current file. There’s also <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">read-regexp</code>,
which adds a bunch of standard suggestions (the ones you saw in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">occur</code>) to
whatever you give it.</p>

<p>That’s the trick behind most of the
<a href="/blog/2026/11/26/smarter-minibuffer-prompts-in-projectile/">smarter prompts</a>
in the upcoming Projectile 3.5. I like this approach a lot, because the
suggestions don’t get in anyone’s way - if you never press <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> the prompt
behaves exactly as it always has.</p>

<h2 id="lazy-suggestions">Lazy Suggestions</h2>

<p>There’s one catch with the list approach - you have to compute all the
suggestions before the prompt shows up, even though most of the time nobody
will ask for them. If they are expensive to compute that’s a problem. Enter
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">minibuffer-default-add-function</code>. Emacs calls it the first time <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> runs out
of default values, and whatever it returns becomes the new list of defaults:</p>

<div class="language-emacs-lisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">minibuffer-with-setup-hook</span>
    <span class="p">(</span><span class="k">lambda</span> <span class="p">()</span>
      <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">setq-local</span> <span class="nv">minibuffer-default-add-function</span>
                  <span class="p">(</span><span class="k">lambda</span> <span class="p">()</span>
                    <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">append</span> <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">my-expensive-guesses</span><span class="p">)</span>
                            <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">minibuffer-default-add-completions</span><span class="p">)))))</span>
  <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">completing-read</span> <span class="s">"Find file: "</span> <span class="nv">files</span><span class="p">))</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">minibuffer-default-add-completions</code> is what this variable is set to out of the
box, and it’s the reason why <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> goes through the completion candidates in
the first place. Here we just put our own guesses in front of them. Keep in mind
that the function gets called with the minibuffer as the current buffer, so if
your guesses depend on the buffer the command was invoked from (e.g. you want
the thing at point) you’ll have to switch to it first.
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">(with-selected-window (minibuffer-selected-window) ...)</code> does the job. That’s
how <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">find-file</code> comes up with the file name at point, by the way, and I’m using
the same trick for Projectile’s <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">projectile-find-file</code>.</p>

<p>That’s all I have for you today. Give <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-n</code> a try the next time Emacs asks you
something - you might be pleasantly surprised! Keep hacking!</p>

